The `mat-hint` in the `mat-form-field` is already designed to provide a hint for the user when they type into the input field. It typically displays the format or type of values that should be entered.

If you want the `mat-hint` to provide more specific or detailed information about the expected format of the values, you can do so by using template literals and string interpolation. This allows you to dynamically display information based on the input value.

Here's an example of how you can modify the `mat-hint` to provide more valuable information:

```html
@if (fieldForm.get("values")) {
  <div class="col-12">
    <mat-form-field class="w-100">
      <mat-label>Values</mat-label>
      <input matInput formControlName="values" />
      <mat-hint>{{formatValues(values)}}</mat-hint>
    </mat-form-field>
  </div>
}
```

In this example, `formatValues(values)` is a function that you might define to format the values based on your specific requirements. For example, if you want to display the values in a comma-separated list, you could define the function like this:

```typescript
// Assuming values is an array of numbers
formatValues(values) {
  return values.join(", ");
}
```

Then, in your Angular component, you would call this function whenever you want to update the `mat-hint` to reflect the current value of `values`.

```typescript
@output
formatValues(values) {
  console.log(values);
  // Update the mat-hint with the formatted values
  this.fieldForm.get("values").setValue(values);
}
```

This approach ensures that the `mat-hint` dynamically updates to reflect the current input value, providing more valuable and relevant information to the user.

